# KineticSandbox v5

**Static malware analysis — evidence-weighted verdicts, without ever running the sample.**

KineticSandbox **never executes** a file and never connects to an address. It only reads
bytes: headers, the import table, embedded layers, script code and signature data, and
turns them into a *reasoned* risk score. The GUI ships in Turkish and English.

```
Version    : v5
File       : kinetic_sandbox.py (single file, ~24,400 lines)
Hunting    : 12 modules · 166 rules · 39 persistence locations · 39 credential targets
Atlas      : 1,429 techniques · 21 artifact families · 110 curated combos
Security   : 9-stage trust layer · 136 contradiction rules · 106 framework+publisher records
Test suite : 621 checks (621/621 passing, ~40 s)
Requires   : customtkinter (required) · pefile, tkinterdnd2 (optional)
Verdicts   : CLEAN < 25 ≤ SUSPICIOUS < 45 ≤ MALICIOUS
ATT&CK     : 152 techniques
```

> ⚠️ Never run a suspicious sample on your real machine. This tool does not launch the
> sample, but keeping it inside an isolated VM is still the right habit.

🇹🇷 Türkçe sürüm: [`README.md`](README.md)

---

## 1) Installation

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt     # customtkinter (required), pefile, tkinterdnd2
python kinetic_sandbox.py           # open the GUI
```

Without `pefile`, PE analysis degrades (the app still runs). Without `tkinterdnd2`,
drag-and-drop is disabled. Python 3.10+ recommended. No other dependencies; network access
happens **only** for the optional VirusTotal lookup, and only the SHA-256 digest is sent.

---

## 2) Quick start

### GUI

```bash
python kinetic_sandbox.py
```

Drag the file onto the window → **▶ STATİK ANALİZ ET** (run static analysis) → walk the
tabs → **💾 RAPORU KAYDET** (save report) or **🗎 JSON DIŞA AKTAR** (export JSON).

| Tab | Contents |
|---|---|
| 📌 ÖZET / SUMMARY | Verdict, score, reasons, strong evidence, **triage block** (kill-chain stages), signature state, family, ATT&CK summary |
| ⚙ DAVRANIŞ / BEHAVIOR | Commands, persistence, registry writes, API behaviour |
| 📡 İLİŞKİLER / RELATIONS | URLs/hosts, IPs, domains, likely dropped files |
| 📂 DOSYA İŞLEMLERİ / FILE OPS | Files read / written / deleted / executed |
| 🎯 ATT&CK | MITRE ATT&CK mapping, family matches, container and shellcode findings |
| 🔬 STATİK VERİ / STATIC | PE headers, sections, import categories, manifest |
| 🔓 DECODE | Automatic layer decoding + **layer summary**, **text filter**, **save layers** + manual decode panel |
| 🔤 STRING'LER / STRINGS | ASCII / Unicode string dump |
| 📋 LOG | Analysis log |
| ⚙ AYARLAR / SETTINGS | **View/change application settings + update log (upd log)** |

#### SETTINGS tab (v4)

| Section | What it does |
|---|---|
| Language | Switches the UI language (TR/EN), applied immediately |
| VirusTotal | Enter the API key, turn the VT lookup on/off (only SHA-256 is sent) |
| Verdict thresholds | `CLEAN < 25 ≤ SUSPICIOUS < 45 ≤ MALICIOUS` and the strong-evidence threshold (30 points) shown **read-only** |
| Limits | Max file size, script characters, search layers, container depth |
| Hunting modules | Turn each of the 12 modules (atlas included) **on/off**; a disabled module does not run |
| Configuration | `~/.kineticsandbox.json` path + **Save / Reload / Open file / Export / Reset to defaults** |
| Update log | Shows `CHANGELOG.md` when present, otherwise the embedded update log; **REFRESH** button |

#### DECODE tab refresh (v4)

- **Layer summary** always at the top: `KATMAN ÖZETİ: 12 katman · Base64: 6, Hex: 3, PS -enc: 2…`
- **Text filter:** type a word such as `url`, `ip`, `base64` or `powershell` and press
  **FİLTRELE**; only matching lines remain and the match count appears in the status bar.
  `Enter` works too.
- **KATMANLARI KAYDET (save layers):** exports every decoded layer as JSON (or `.txt`).

### Command line

```bash
python kinetic_sandbox.py --cli sample.exe                    # single file
python kinetic_sandbox.py --cli samples/ --recursive          # batch scan
python kinetic_sandbox.py --cli samples/ --json out.json --report out.txt \
       --min-level malicious --quiet                           # automation
```

| Option | Description |
|---|---|
| `--cli` | Command-line mode instead of the GUI (automatic when a sample argument is given) |
| `--json PATH` | JSON output (one object for a single file, an array for many) — includes the `hunting` block |
| `--report PATH` | Human-readable full report (with the THREAT HUNTING section) |
| `--lang {tr,en}` | Report language |
| `--recursive` | Recurse into folders |
| `--max-files N` | Batch scan cap (default 500) |
| `--min-level LEVEL` | Only show `clean` / `suspicious` / `malicious` and above |
| `--quiet` / `--verbose` / `--no-color` | Quiet summary / reasons + HUNT and ATLAS dump / no ANSI colour |
| `--trust-profile NAME` | `auto` (default) / `triage` (no demotion) / `source` / `forensics` |
| `--trust-policy PATH` | Trust policy JSON file |
| `--audit-log` | Write verdicts to the hash-chained audit trail |
| `--trust-doctor` | Trust-layer diagnosis: benchmark + table health + calibration (no file needed) |

Each line now also carries hunting data, and `--verbose` adds the atlas line:

```
MALICIOUS  100/100  a.ps1   71 B  aile=-  attack=3  hunt=32.1  asama=3
    ATLAS eslesme=12/1429 puan=80.0 zincir=execution → persistence → evasion
```

**Exit codes:** `0` no malware · `1` usage/IO error · `2` at least one MALICIOUS ·
`3` suspicious only. (Designed for CI and batch scripts.)

---

## 3) Decision model

The score is built on **evidence weight**, not on "does a category exist":

```
CLEAN       :   0 – 24
SUSPICIOUS  :  25 – 44
MALICIOUS   :  45 – 100   (always with at least one STRONG EVIDENCE item)
```

- **Ordinary APIs earn no points.** About 180 APIs such as `Sleep`, `CreateFile`,
  `LoadLibrary`, `InternetOpen` and `RegSetValueEx` are listed for information only — every
  Windows binary contains them. (The old engine called a signed Chrome installer 75/100
  MALICIOUS because of this; today it is 0.)
- **No MALICIOUS verdict without strong evidence.** The score is clamped at 44 and the
  report states *"capped at SUSPICIOUS because no strong evidence was found"*.
- **Decisive strong evidence:** a single ≥30-point strong item, or two separate strong
  items, makes the verdict MALICIOUS directly.
- **Evidence accumulation:** 4+ independent evidence groups (API, text, decode, integrity,
  structure, IOC, family, **hunting**) combined with ≥55 raw points promotes the file to
  MALICIOUS.
- **v4 hunting evidence** is added as its own group: the 10 strongest hunting findings are
  scored, category caps apply, and only high-signal categories (injection, ransomware,
  credential, C2, persistence, defence evasion) can produce **strong evidence**.
- **A signature never erases evidence:** a tampered signature ⇒ MALICIOUS; a signed file
  that injects into another process stays suspicious/malicious.
- **v5 atlas evidence** joins the same pool but is deliberately conservative: see §4.2.

### 3.1 Trust layer and contradiction resolution (v4-security)

The rule engine asks "what is in the file"; the trust layer asks **"what is this string
doing here?"** The same string can be three different things:

```
powershell -w hidden -enc SQBFAFgA      → an executed command (evidence)
$a = "powershell -w hidden -enc" ascii  → a YARA string (rule text)
`powershell -w hidden -enc`             → a documentation example (quote)
```

The layer runs in 9 stages and **sits above the rule engine as the decision authority**:

| # | Stage | Job |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `FileFingerprint` | Bytes/entropy/printability/code ratio, line counts, decode layers, technique density |
| 2 | `HazardCatalog` | 1,096 hazardous strings split into 12 families (ransomware, injection, credential, C2, persistence, evasion…) |
| 3 | `SelfReferenceGuard` | Is this file KineticSandbox itself / a copy of it? (16 markers, confidence ratio) |
| 4 | `ContextClassifier` | Context: executable payload / script / source code / rule set / documentation / test / dataset |
| 5 | `HazardContextAnalyzer` | Counts each string as **exec** or **catalog** context; `exec_ratio`, `catalog_ratio` |
| 6 | `ContradictionResolver` | 136 contradiction records: when evidence A and B are both true, a policy applies (`a_wins`, `b_wins`, `cap_suspicious`, `cap_clean`, `merge`, `require_strong`) |
| 7 | `TrustScorer` | 0–100 confidence: signature, known publisher, safe directory, context, self-reference, framework baseline, tampered signature (−), masquerade (−) |
| 8 | `VerdictArbiter` | Final verdict: whitelist → test data → self-reference → detector corpus → documentation → test directory → unresolved contradiction → absence of execution context |
| 9 | `TrustPolicyStore` / `TrustEngine` | User policy (hash/path/name/publisher) and orchestration |

**Ground rule:** a demotion never rests on a single weak indicator. Reaching CLEAN requires
the file to be our own source, to sit in a rule/source-code context (high catalog ratio +
low execution ratio + no real payload), to be test data, or to be whitelisted in policy. A
real injection/driver/ransomware chain is **never** suppressed, in any profile.

When a demotion happens:

- the verdict and score drop (`verdict_downgraded` appears in the JSON),
- the reason is appended to the report as an **informational** (0-point) evidence line,
- the contradiction count and unresolved contradictions are reported,
- a `trust_layer` block is written to the JSON (profile, context, trust score, winning verdict).

```json
"verdict_downgraded": {"from": "malicious", "from_score": 100,
                       "to": "clean", "to_score": 0, "reason": "self_reference"}
```

**Trust profiles** (`--trust-profile`):

| Profile | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `auto` | Decides context itself, corpus demotion enabled (default) |
| `triage` | Sample triage: **no demotion at all**, self-recognition disabled |
| `source` | Writing source code / rules: corpus demotion on, contradiction resolution on |
| `forensics` | Forensic review: no demotion, but contradictions and the evidence ledger are reported |

### 3.2 Score display

Raw evidence points can exceed 1000 on large samples (553 on a self-scan). That does not
mean "1000 = very bad"; it means many indicators were found. Therefore:

- `evidence_points` **caps the raw score at 200** (`evidence_points_capped` flag),
- the report's **EVIDENCE BREAKDOWN** table shows the raw score group by group (API, text,
  decode, integrity, structure, IOC, family, hunting) as percentages,
- the verdict band is always on a 0–100 scale.

---

## 4) Detection modules

### 4.1 v4 — threat hunting (11 modules, 166 rules)

| Module | What it catches |
|---|---|
| **Rule engine** | 166 YARA-like rules: required string + additional-string threshold, category point caps, strong-evidence flag, ATT&CK label |
| **Persistence hunter** | 39 locations: Run/RunOnce/Policies, Winlogon, AppInit_DLLs, IFEO, COM/CLSID, LSA packages, BootExecute, services, scheduled tasks, WMI subscriptions, cron/`@reboot`, systemd, `ld.so.preload`, LaunchAgent/Daemon, `authorized_keys`, `rc.local`, XDG autostart |
| **Credential hunter** | 39 targets: LSASS, SAM, NTDS.dit, browser passwords/cookies/Autofill/Local State, wallets (wallet.dat, Exodus, MetaMask…), Telegram `tdata`, Discord/Steam sessions, KeePass/1Password/Bitwarden, SSH/AWS/Azure/Kubernetes/Docker keys, DPAPI, keylogger APIs, clipboard, cloud metadata (`169.254.169.254`) |
| **LOLBAS chains** | 60+ binary/switch combinations (certutil, bitsadmin, mshta, regsvr32 `scrobj.dll`, rundll32 `javascript:`, installutil, msiexec, wmic, forfiles, pcalua…) plus a **download → execute → persist → hide** chain graph |
| **Defence evasion** | AMSI/ETW patching, Defender exclusions & service disabling, firewall, event-log clearing, PowerShell logging teardown, UAC-bypass binaries, VM/sandbox and analysis-tool artifacts, anti-debug APIs |
| **Injection analysis** | 11 API clusters (classic remote thread, APC, hollowing, thread hijack, section mapping, doppelgänging, callback, self-injection…) — checked in both the import table and the text; multiple techniques = extra bonus |
| **Ransomware** | Shadow-copy deletion, recovery disabling, ransom-note wording/filenames, extension rewriting, encrypt-in-loop, backup service stopping, disk wiping; 3+ signals = chain |
| **C2 / exfiltration** | Telegram Bot API, Discord webhooks, paste sites, dynamic DNS, Tor, RMM tools (AnyDesk, ScreenConnect…), tunnels (ngrok, cloudflared, chisel), SMTP/FTP/cloud exfil, C2 panel paths, raw TCP sockets |
| **Packer detection** | UPX/ASPack/MPRESS/Themida/VMProtect/PECompact/Enigma + .NET (ConfuserEx, SmartAssembly), PyInstaller/Nuitka/Go/Rust/AutoIt markers, section entropy, few imports + high entropy, TLS callbacks |
| **Obfuscation measurement** | base64 decoding, dynamic execution, char-code, string concatenation, compression, escape sequences, reversed/split strings and multi-layer decode count |
| **Triage** | Kill-chain stage coverage (recon → delivery → execution → persistence → evasion → credential → collection → c2 → impact), strongest findings, category distribution, ATT&CK techniques |

### 4.2 v5 — technique atlas (12th hunting module, 1,429 techniques)

The 166 rules catch "high-signal behaviour"; the **atlas** scans a broad technique catalog
at *artifact level*. Every technique carries a **trigger** (string, command, API or key) and
belongs to one of 21 families:

| Category | Families | Coverage |
|---|---|---|
| persistence | `persistence_registry`, `persistence_path`, `persistence_schtask`, `persistence_service`, `linux_persistence`, `macos_persistence` | Run/Winlogon/IFEO, services, tasks, cron/systemd, LaunchAgent/Daemon |
| credential | `credential_artifact` | LSASS/SAM/NTDS, browser databases, wallets, SSH/cloud keys, DPAPI |
| execution | `execution_lolbin`, `office_macro` | LOLBAS binaries, Office macro/VBA layers |
| defence evasion | `evasion_artifact` | AMSI/ETW, Defender exclusions, log clearing, VM/analysis artifacts |
| injection | `injection_api` | 11 API clusters and text patterns |
| C2 | `c2_tool`, `network_artifact` | RMM, tunnels, webhooks, paste sites, Tor, raw sockets |
| discovery/collection | `discovery_command`, `collection_artifact` | `whoami`/`net`/`systeminfo`, archiving, clipboard, screenshots |
| impact | `impact_artifact` | Shadow copies, backup deletion, ransom notes, disk wiping |
| driver/firmware | `driver_byovd`, `firmware_artifact` | BYOVD drivers, boot/UEFI commands |
| cloud/container | `cloud_api`, `container_k8s` | IMDS, `aws`/`gcloud`/`az`, `docker`/`kubectl`/`privileged` |
| browser | `browser_artifact` | Cookie/session/extension theft paths |

**False-positive protection (the most critical part of this release):**

1. **A single artifact never scores.** A family is not scored until it sees at least **2**
   distinct artifact matches (or a curated strong combination); a lone match is shown in the
   report only.
2. **Category caps** (e.g. discovery 12, persistence 34) — families sharing a category cannot
   exceed the cap together.
3. **PE context:** inside a PE an API name is behavioural evidence only when it appears in
   the **import table**; appearing in the string table earns nothing (otherwise PyInstaller
   launchers were inflated by the `isdebuggerpresent` string). Such families are additionally
   capped at 6 points in binary context.
4. **Legitimate management-script scale-down:** when only persistence/discovery/collection
   families match (a typical installer or backup script), the total contribution is scaled to
   12 points. Credential, impact, C2, injection, driver or evasion evidence disables the
   scale-down.
5. **110 curated combinations** outweigh individual matches (e.g. `certutil -urlcache` +
   `bitsadmin /transfer` + `rundll32 javascript:`).

**Chain graph (`TechniqueGraph`):** matched families are placed on the 9-stage kill chain
(recon → delivery → execution → persistence → evasion → credential → collection → c2 →
impact); edges are built from ordered matches. Coverage of 3/5/7 stages grants a **4/9/12
point** chain bonus. A single-stage file gets no bonus — so a text document that mentions
everything cannot produce a high score.

**Contradiction catalog 62 → 136.** The 74 new records fall into two groups: 43
atlas/graph/telemetry contradictions (`atlas:*`, `graph:*`, `telemetry:*`) and 31 review
contradictions (`atlas_evasion_vs_binary_signature`, `persistence_only_script`,
`atlas_impact_vs_installer_repair`, `score_inflation_multi_module`, `self_scan_graph_noise`…).
The resolver now **aggregates prefix tokens** (`atlas:`, `reason:`, `hunt_rule:`) per family,
so it never depends on a single literal reason id.

### 4.3 v4-security — trust and contradiction layer

| Component | What it does |
|---|---|
| **`FileFingerprint`** | Measurable fingerprint: bytes, entropy, printable ratio, code/line ratio, language guess, PE body, script markers, decode layers, technique density |
| **`HazardCatalog`** | 1,096 hazardous strings in 12 families; checks whether each family is actually *used* |
| **`SelfReferenceGuard`** | 16 markers answer "is this file KineticSandbox itself?" (copies, renamed files, version drift) |
| **`ContextClassifier`** | Payload / script / source code / rule set / security tool / documentation / test / dataset / container classes + confidence |
| **`HazardContextAnalyzer`** | Counts each string as **exec** or **catalog** context; line-level classification (`$a = "..."`, YAML lists, inline backticks, assignment lines) |
| **`ContradictionResolver`** | 136 records; each carries a resolution policy and a severity; unresolved ones are reported as `unresolved` |
| **`TrustScorer`** | 18 weighted indicators: valid signature, known publisher, safe directory, context, self-reference, catalog context, framework baseline; negatives: tampered signature, suspicious directory, unsigned, sandbox artifacts |
| **`VerdictArbiter`** | Verdict arbiter: attempts demotion step by step and must pass "real payload / real injection / real driver / real ransomware" checks at every step |
| **`TrustPolicyStore`** | `~/.kineticsandbox_trust.json`: hash / path / filename / publisher whitelist, hash/path/name blacklist; written with `0600` permissions |
| **`BenignFrameworkBaseline`** | Recognises 23 software frameworks (.NET, Electron, Unity, PyInstaller, Go, Rust, Qt, JVM, CEF…): legitimate framework strings do not create false positives |
| **`PublisherTrustInspector`** | 83 publishers (Microsoft, Sysinternals, Adobe, Google, security products, RMM tools…) + **masquerade** check: publisher name present but signature missing/tampered ⇒ masquerade suspicion |
| **`EvidenceLedger`** | Evidence chain: every item gets an `E01…` id with its source (rule engine, hunting, catalog, context, trust) and weight |
| **`TrustAuditTrail`** | Hash-chained JSONL decision log (`~/.kineticsandbox_audit.jsonl`); tampering breaks the chain; enabled with `--audit-log` |
| **`TrustPolicyRules`** | Policy validation/normalisation/deduplication; a broken policy is reported, never silently swallowed |
| **`TrustCalibrator`** | Demotion statistics and calibration suggestions from the audit trail |
| **`SignatureHealth`** | Health of its own tables: rule ids, rule shape, contradiction records, profile fields, i18n parity, family/ATT&CK tables, catalog, framework/publisher tables |
| **`TrustBenchmark`** | 5 synthetic scenarios exercise context/execution separation without touching the filesystem (SETTINGS → **SELF-TEST**) |
| **`EscalationAdvisor`** | Verdict-driven action advice (urgent / high / medium / info) plus contradiction notes such as "high trust but malicious verdict" |
| **`ScoreBreakdown` / `RationaleExplainer`** | Group-level distribution of the raw score and the verdict translated into plain language (TR+EN) |

### 4.4 v3 base layers

| Module | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Decode engine** | Base64 / Base64URL / line-wrapped / missing padding / nested, PowerShell `-EncodedCommand`, hex (5 notations), `[char]`, `fromCharCode`, `Chr()` chains, split-string concatenation, line continuations, URL `%XX`, ROT13/reverse, GZip/ZLib/Deflate, XOR guessing |
| **Authenticode** | Compares the digest in the certificate table with the digest computed from the file; catches tampering **and data appended after the certificate table** |
| **Driver / BYOVD** | `NtLoadDriver`, `SeLoadDriverPrivilege`, `SERVICE_KERNEL_DRIVER`, `.sys` paths, known vulnerable driver names, physical memory access |
| **Family signature DB** | 31 families (Cobalt Strike, Meterpreter, njRAT, RedLine, AgentTesla, Emotet, QakBot, LockBit, WannaCry, Mimikatz, XMRig…). *Anchor + context* model: mentioning a family name is not enough |
| **ATT&CK mapping** | 152 techniques; every match comes with its evidence |
| **Shellcode heuristics** | PEB access, API hashing, GetPC, syscall density; in PEs only the overlay |
| **Container opener** | Opens ZIP/GZip **in memory**; classifies child PE/scripts; recognises NSIS/Inno/PyInstaller/.NET/AutoIt frameworks |
| **Embedded PE** | PE carving and detection of PE/drivers inside resources |
| **XOR hunt** | 4 × 128 KB windows, 255 keys; hidden driver/credential strings |
| **Reputation DB** | Local hash black/whitelist (`~/.kineticsandbox_iocs.json`) |
| **VirusTotal** | Optional, off by default; only the SHA-256 is sent |
| **File operations** | Files read / written / deleted / executed + notable targets |

### False-positive protections

- 14–15 real system binaries and a signed Microsoft sample stay **CLEAN**.
- **Its own source is CLEAN:** scanning `kinetic_sandbox.py` returns CLEAN 0 (self-reference).
- **Rule/documentation corpus is CLEAN:** YARA, Sigma and ransomware research notes are
  clean; real loader/ransomware scripts stay MALICIOUS.
- **Legitimate installers are CLEAN:** `curl … | bash` from official distribution hosts,
  `systemctl enable`, `msiexec /i`, backups (`pg_dump | gzip`) and API clients.
- Signed + known publisher + no strong evidence ⇒ CLEAN.
- The `\Registry\…\Services` string alone is **not** driver evidence (installers read it).
- An unsigned "Microsoft Corporation" claim is not MALICIOUS on its own (SUSPICIOUS).
- **Download-context trust:** downloads from official distribution hosts (NodeSource,
  rustup, Docker, Homebrew, PyPI, Chocolatey, npm, Google/Microsoft update) or internal
  addresses neutralise download/execution **and persistence** evidence — a Node.js/Docker
  install via `curl … | bash` stays CLEAN. The triage score is scaled by the same factor.
- **v4 rule engine:** a single weak indicator earns nothing (0.6 factor), every category is
  capped, and even a "strong"-flagged rule is not strong evidence without enough matches.
- **v4 injection:** one API is not enough; the whole cluster is required (e.g.
  `VirtualAllocEx` + `WriteProcessMemory` + `CreateRemoteThread`).
- Family anchors shorter than 10 characters require word boundaries (no accidental matches
  inside random base64 noise).
- **v5 atlas:** minimum 2 matches per family, category caps, the PE import gate, the
  management-script scale-down and single-artifact non-scoring (see §4.2).

---

## 5) Configuration and data files

| Path | Contents |
|---|---|
| `~/.kineticsandbox.json` | Language, VirusTotal key/on-off, **disabled hunting modules** (`hunt_disabled`), version |
| `~/.kineticsandbox_iocs.json` | Local reputation DB — hash black/whitelist. Falls back to the `iocs.example.json` template, which the GUI can create with one click |
| `iocs.example.json` | Template + DragonBurn example (VT 52/71) |
| `~/.kineticsandbox_trust.json` | **Trust policy**: profile + hash/path/name/publisher whitelist, hash/path/name blacklist (`0600`) |
| `~/.kineticsandbox_audit.jsonl` | **Audit trail** (optional): hash-chained verdict records, rotated at 512 KB |

Example `~/.kineticsandbox.json`:

```json
{
  "language": "tr",
  "vt_api_key": "",
  "vt_enabled": false,
  "hunt_disabled": ["c2", "packer"],
  "trust_profile": "auto",
  "trust_policy": "/home/user/.kineticsandbox_trust.json",
  "version": "5"
}
```

The VirusTotal lookup sends **only the hash**, never uploads the file, and is off by default
(`vt_api_key` + `vt_enabled`). The **EXPORT SETTINGS** button masks the key as `***`.

---

## 6) Limits (the honest ones)

- **Static only:** the sample is not executed, no URL is contacted, no DNS query is made.
- **Certificate chains are not validated:** only the Authenticode *digest* is compared
  (chain/trust-store validation is the operating system's job). A validly signed malware
  looks "signed"; behaviour decides the verdict.
- **Container support is ZIP + GZip.** RAR, 7-Zip, CAB, MSI/OLE and NSIS bodies are
  *recognised and reported*, not extracted.
- **Family matching is text-based:** packed samples without anchors will not match; ATT&CK
  evidence, hunting rules and structural rules fill that gap.
- **Hunting modules are text/import based:** they cannot see strings built dynamically in
  memory; on a single fully encrypted layer only entropy/packer indicators remain.
- **The XOR hunt is bounded** (4 windows) and the shellcode heuristic inspects only the
  overlay in PEs.
- **Disabling a module in SETTINGS** affects only that session's analysis; a disabled module
  is listed in the report's "EXCLUDED MODULES" line.
- **The atlas is an artifact catalog, not a crystal ball:** it scores families, not intent.
  A file can legitimately contain two persistence strings (see the management-script
  scale-down), and a novel sample can hide behind artifacts nobody has catalogued yet.
- **The trust layer is a *context* layer, not magic:** a rule-like malware that really
  stores its text in executable form (exec context) is not demoted; a file that looks
  entirely like a catalog could theoretically slip through. When in doubt use the `triage`
  profile — it never demotes.
- **The audit trail is local** (`~/.kineticsandbox_audit.jsonl`, off by default) and only
  provides *tamper detection*; it does not ship decisions to a remote log server.
- **This is a heuristic tool, not an authority.** Signed malware, new obfuscation and
  fileless techniques can always evade it. Use an isolated VM for in-depth review.

---

## 7) Tests

```bash
python test_kinetic_sandbox.py              # 621 checks, ~40 s
python test_kinetic_sandbox.py --verbose    # detail for every check
python kinetic_sandbox.py --trust-doctor    # trust-layer diagnosis
```

| Block | Checks | Coverage |
|---|---|---|
| A | 56 | Decode engine: every encoding, nested layers, 500 random inputs |
| B | 38 | False positives: 15 benign files, 10 PE scenarios, real binaries |
| C | 47 | Detection: 8 script families + 12 decisive PE behaviours, threshold/limit rules |
| D | 37 | Authenticode digest, tampering and **appended data**, manifest, reputation, VT |
| E | 40 | TR+EN reports, key parity, JSON output |
| F | 22 | Limits: empty/small/large files, missing path, score range, timing |
| G | 32 | Family DB integrity, false-match protection, ATT&CK |
| H | 33 | Containers, shellcode, file operations |
| I | 21 | CLI: exit codes, folder scans, JSON/report output |
| J | 49 | Decode regressions, timing limits, calibration and the benign control set |
| **K** | **87** | **v4: rule engine, persistence, credential, LOLBAS, defence evasion, injection, ransomware, C2, packer, obfuscation, triage, SETTINGS/DECODE, FP and performance** |
| **L** | **97** | **v4-security: self-recognition, context/catalog separation, contradiction resolution, profiles, policy (white/blacklist), audit trail and chain verification, framework baseline, publisher/masquerade, evidence ledger, escalation, report/CLI, performance** |
| **M** | **62** | **v5: the 1,429-technique atlas, family thresholds and category caps, curated combinations, PE import gate, chain graph and bonus, the expanded contradiction catalog, atlas report/CLI/JSON, self-scan cleanliness and performance** |

If `/tmp/sigcheck.exe` (a real signed Microsoft binary) is present, 6 more checks run;
otherwise they are skipped silently — none of them counts as "failed".

**Performance (on this machine):** 447 KB signed PE ≈ 0.6 s · 3 MB script ≈ 2.4 s ·
133 KB single-line Base64 full analysis ≈ 0.9 s (decode 0.06 s) · 3-file folder ≈ 0.2 s ·
self-scan with the atlas active ≈ 3.8 s.

---

## 8) FAQ

### 8.1 Trust layer (v4-security)

- **"The tool used to call its own file MALICIOUS; now it says CLEAN. Can I trust that?"**
  The trust layer looks at three independent signals: (1) `SelfReferenceGuard` verifies with
  16 markers that the file *is* KineticSandbox, (2) `ContextClassifier` classifies the
  context as "rule set / security tool", (3) `HazardContextAnalyzer` measures that 90%+ of
  the hazardous strings live in *catalog* (rule table) context. If any of the three is
  missing, no demotion happens. Moreover, if there is a real PE body / injection / driver /
  ransomware chain, `VerdictArbiter` refuses to demote — so a real payload hidden inside our
  own source is still caught.
- **"Could real malware pretend to be a rule file and slip through?"** For a rule-file
  context to reach CLEAN, execution context must be ≤ 20%, the catalog ratio ≥ 45%, and the
  file must carry no PE/script payload. A loader line such as
  `IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadString(...)` is in *exec* context, so no demotion
  happens (block L locks this in the test suite).
- **"Which profile should I pick?"** `auto` for sample analysis. `source` when scanning a
  rule set or the tool's own source. `triage` when you never want demotion (e.g. production
  decisions). `forensics` for forensic review (no demotion, full contradiction and evidence
  reporting).

### 8.2 Atlas (v5)

- **"I scanned a signed exe and the atlas matched 5 techniques — why is it CLEAN?"**
  Because matching is not scoring. Most of those matches never reach the 2-artifact family
  threshold, the API families only count import-table entries inside a PE, and every
  category has a cap. The report still shows what was seen, so you can inspect it.
- **"Why does a research note full of ransomware commands stay CLEAN?"**
  Context. The file is a document, the strings sit in prose, the category cap applies, and
  the chain bonus requires real execution/persistence pairing. Chains are built from
  families that are *scored*, not from every mention.
- **"My file only matched the persistence family and got 12 points — is the scale-down too
  aggressive?"** Check the family list in the report. If credential, impact, C2, injection or
  driver evidence appears anywhere, the scale-down is switched off entirely. If you still
  disagree, use `--trust-profile triage` and read the raw group scores.
- **"Can I turn the atlas off?"** Yes: **SETTINGS → HUNTING MODULES** → uncheck `atlas`.
  The verdict then rests on the other 11 modules and the report lists it under
  "EXCLUDED MODULES".

**VirusTotal says 50/70, KineticSandbox says 30 — why?**
The two numbers measure different things: VT counts "how many engines flagged it",
KineticSandbox measures "how strong the evidence is". If the gap is large there is a real
miss. v4 introduced the hunting layer for exactly this gap (166 rules + 12 modules + the
1,429-technique atlas + kill-chain triage), and decode layers can now be filtered and
exported. If you still see a gap, share the **EXPORT JSON** output; we can calibrate against
that sample.

**One of the hunting modules is noisy — can I turn it off?**
Yes: **⚙ SETTINGS → HUNTING MODULES**, uncheck the box and press **SAVE SETTINGS**. A
disabled module does not run, it is listed in the report as "EXCLUDED MODULES", and the
verdict is made from the remaining evidence.

**Where do I find the update log?**
On the right side of the **⚙ SETTINGS** tab. If a `CHANGELOG.md` sits next to the app it is
read from disk, otherwise the embedded log is shown; **REFRESH** re-reads the file.

**The decode output is huge — how do I narrow it?**
Type a word such as `url`, `ip`, `base64` or `powershell` into the DECODE filter field and
press **FILTER** (or `Enter`). Only matching lines remain; **CLEAR FILTER** restores the old
view and **SAVE LAYERS** writes the result to a file.

**Why is a signed file reported as MALICIOUS?**
A signature only means "this file has not changed *since it was signed*". If the content
changed after signing (tampering or an appended payload) the signature is invalid and the
file becomes MALICIOUS.

**Can I whitelist a file?**
Add its SHA-256 to `whitelist` in `~/.kineticsandbox_iocs.json`; the verdict becomes CLEAN (0).

**The GUI will not open.**
`pip install customtkinter` is required. On a server/SSH box use
`python kinetic_sandbox.py --cli <file>` instead.

